WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.033 --> 00:00:02.367
-Welcome back to "Late Night."

00:00:02.367 --> 00:00:04.033
We're here with our friend
Natasha Lyonne, everybody.

00:00:04.033 --> 00:00:06.534
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:06.534 --> 00:00:11.434
You shot this show all over,
including Las Vegas, New Mexico.

00:00:11.434 --> 00:00:13.067
How does someone like you --
-If you say so.

00:00:13.067 --> 00:00:15.000
-A city person like yourself,

00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:17.467
how did you feel
with the desert environment?

00:00:17.467 --> 00:00:19.667
Did you enjoy working there?

00:00:19.667 --> 00:00:24.701
-Um, so, you know, I...

00:00:24.701 --> 00:00:27.133
[ Laughter ]

00:00:27.133 --> 00:00:28.701
You guys don't know my life.

00:00:28.701 --> 00:00:33.100
-You're reacting like this is
a gotcha question.

00:00:33.100 --> 00:00:34.400
-First of all, I will say,

00:00:34.400 --> 00:00:38.000
it's always surprising to me
to hear the locations.

00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:41.434
I know I was there,
but it's like a memory.

00:00:41.434 --> 00:00:49.267
But I -- Yeah, I don't love
heat, as a rule.

00:00:49.267 --> 00:00:52.601
You know,
I don't mind dry desert heat.

00:00:52.601 --> 00:00:56.801
I think I like the sort of --
the landscape, the expanse,

00:00:56.801 --> 00:01:02.300
uh-huh,
but I don't love my hair.

00:01:02.300 --> 00:01:04.434
It's a lot of hair, Seth.
It's like a sable coat.

00:01:04.434 --> 00:01:08.067
And what comes with that,
you know --

00:01:08.067 --> 00:01:10.133
You know I also --
I write, I direct,

00:01:10.133 --> 00:01:12.234
I produce like gangbusters.

00:01:12.234 --> 00:01:15.334
-Oh, yeah.
One of our foremost producers.

00:01:15.334 --> 00:01:18.367
-Well, and -- I'm like
a young Bob Evans figure,

00:01:18.367 --> 00:01:20.400
but we know this.

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:22.501
The point is, when acting,

00:01:22.501 --> 00:01:25.767
you've got -- There's a lot of
touching involved,

00:01:25.767 --> 00:01:30.000
which is -- I like touch,
but by choice.

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:32.167
-Yeah. I think
that's pretty much

00:01:32.167 --> 00:01:33.567
where everybody
is landing these days.

00:01:33.567 --> 00:01:34.701
-Yes.
-Yeah.

00:01:34.701 --> 00:01:38.167
-If we've learned
one thing recently.

00:01:38.167 --> 00:01:41.534
And I'm like a cat that way.

00:01:41.534 --> 00:01:45.067
But, you know, such is
the lot of the actres-s-s-,

00:01:45.067 --> 00:01:47.367
which is how it's pronounced.

00:01:47.367 --> 00:01:48.467
A lot of people say it wrong.

00:01:48.467 --> 00:01:49.701
-Yeah, you do --
You put three S's on it.

00:01:49.701 --> 00:01:51.334
-Yes. Yes.

00:01:51.334 --> 00:01:54.801
And, so, there's a lot of
extra touch involved

00:01:54.801 --> 00:01:58.868
in the heat, and, you know,
so I don't love --

00:01:58.868 --> 00:02:03.200
My dream is to be
a winter actress,

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:07.334
only because then I could --

00:02:07.334 --> 00:02:10.767
But I'm fine to be
a summer writer or director.

00:02:10.767 --> 00:02:12.100
-Okay, so you'll sort of work --

00:02:12.100 --> 00:02:14.200
You'll do sort of behind
the camera in the summer months

00:02:14.200 --> 00:02:18.200
and then on-screen
for "Zhivago" and the like,

00:02:18.200 --> 00:02:19.701
the great winter works.

00:02:19.701 --> 00:02:22.868
-First of all, exactly, Seth.

00:02:22.868 --> 00:02:25.167
Like, those hats and
Julie Christie --

00:02:25.167 --> 00:02:26.367
that's a picture.

00:02:26.367 --> 00:02:29.567
-That's what you want.
-That's a winter picture.

00:02:29.567 --> 00:02:31.467
That's why you
leave the house for acting.

00:02:31.467 --> 00:02:33.000
-Now, you --
Because "Russian Doll,"

00:02:33.000 --> 00:02:35.367
which is a wonderful show,
you know, a show you created,

00:02:35.367 --> 00:02:37.434
you shot that how close to
your actual apartment?

00:02:37.434 --> 00:02:40.767
-Right. So, yeah, you know,
I run it,

00:02:40.767 --> 00:02:43.000
so, you know, it shoots
two blocks from the house

00:02:43.000 --> 00:02:48.667
and in the dead of winter,
in the middle of the night.

00:02:48.667 --> 00:02:52.901
And I wear one outfit,
and it's a black overcoat.

00:02:52.901 --> 00:02:54.701
So, something to think about.

00:02:54.701 --> 00:02:58.367
-Now, you've got --
You did go to school thinking

00:02:58.367 --> 00:03:01.968
you would do film stuff,
but you did not stay

00:03:01.968 --> 00:03:03.033
through-out.

00:03:03.033 --> 00:03:04.634
-Yes. That's how
that's pronounced.

00:03:04.634 --> 00:03:06.300
-Through-out.
-Yes.

00:03:06.300 --> 00:03:08.033
-Well, that's like how
a professor would say it.

00:03:08.033 --> 00:03:09.701
"Natasha, your grades.

00:03:09.701 --> 00:03:12.000
We're not sure you're
going to stay a-through-out."

00:03:12.000 --> 00:03:16.100
-I see you have been to college.

00:03:16.100 --> 00:03:19.167
So, yeah --
So, here's what happened.

00:03:19.167 --> 00:03:22.100
So, I was like what? 15, 16?

00:03:22.100 --> 00:03:24.734
And I said,
"Alright, school, I get it.

00:03:24.734 --> 00:03:30.534
I'll try for one, for Tisch,
and then I'll go there.

00:03:30.534 --> 00:03:32.133
I'll be a film and philosophy
double major,

00:03:32.133 --> 00:03:33.434
if they accept me."

00:03:33.434 --> 00:03:35.901
And I write this thing,
T.S. Eliot, whatever,

00:03:35.901 --> 00:03:39.901
and I -- They send me
back some sort of a note.

00:03:39.901 --> 00:03:42.667
"You're hired"
or whatever colleges say.

00:03:42.667 --> 00:03:45.234
So I show up there.
-"You're hired."

00:03:45.234 --> 00:03:47.334
When I got into college,
it was a post-it note that said,

00:03:47.334 --> 00:03:50.234
"You're hired."

00:03:50.234 --> 00:03:53.834
-So, I show up and I say,
"Great, I'm 16."

00:03:53.834 --> 00:03:56.667
I think they were more 18 or so.
-Yeah.

00:03:56.667 --> 00:03:58.200
Historically,
that's usually what they are.

00:03:58.200 --> 00:03:59.334
-Then good for them.

00:03:59.334 --> 00:04:02.400
And I -- Then they put me
in this class,

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:05.133
and it's Cinema Studies 101.

00:04:05.133 --> 00:04:07.367
And, you know, it was like,

00:04:07.367 --> 00:04:08.667
"We're going to watch
'Apocalypse Now.'"

00:04:08.667 --> 00:04:10.467
And so I went right to
the dean of admissions

00:04:10.467 --> 00:04:13.834
and I said, "Now, listen,
I have seen 'Apocalypse Now.'

00:04:13.834 --> 00:04:15.100
You know, I've read
'Heart of Darkness.'

00:04:15.100 --> 00:04:16.734
I've seen the documentary
'Hearts of Darkness.'

00:04:16.734 --> 00:04:18.267
I loved it.

00:04:18.267 --> 00:04:22.334
The horror,
the horror of my tuition"

00:04:22.334 --> 00:04:24.367
is what I said.

00:04:24.367 --> 00:04:29.033
And I said, "If you think
I'm going to pay

00:04:29.033 --> 00:04:32.267
to hang out with
these teenagers and talk about

00:04:32.267 --> 00:04:34.767
'Apocalypse Now,' -- If you
haven't seen 'Apocalypse Now'

00:04:34.767 --> 00:04:37.567
by this age, why are you
in film school, honey?"

00:04:37.567 --> 00:04:40.200
Like, so, my dream was,

00:04:40.200 --> 00:04:42.868
I was going to read all this,
you know, philosophy.

00:04:42.868 --> 00:04:44.434
I was going to write these
sort of, like,

00:04:44.434 --> 00:04:46.934
Bergman, but comedies,
and direct them.

00:04:46.934 --> 00:04:49.534
And, anyway, I tried to,
you know,

00:04:49.534 --> 00:04:52.667
retroactively negotiate
my way into a scholarship.

00:04:52.667 --> 00:04:54.701
They wouldn't give it to me.
-Yeah. It's tough.

00:04:54.701 --> 00:04:55.834
Once you've already
written them a check,

00:04:55.834 --> 00:04:56.934
they tend not to --

00:04:56.934 --> 00:04:58.934
-I never did is the point.
-Oh, gotcha.

00:04:58.934 --> 00:05:00.467
-I left.

00:05:00.467 --> 00:05:03.667
And, instead, I --
There's a great education

00:05:03.667 --> 00:05:06.767
available to you in this city
at the Film Forum,

00:05:06.767 --> 00:05:10.300
and, also,
in the back of The Times,

00:05:10.300 --> 00:05:13.667
there will be things like YMCAs,
where you can go

00:05:13.667 --> 00:05:15.801
in the middle of the day
with just a few --

00:05:15.801 --> 00:05:18.367
like, a scattered bunch of
very old people.

00:05:18.367 --> 00:05:20.934
And on a little
foldout metal chair,

00:05:20.934 --> 00:05:23.567
I would watch, like,
all the great noir films,

00:05:23.567 --> 00:05:24.801
and I loved it.

00:05:24.801 --> 00:05:27.834
-Yeah. Did you first see
"Gaslight" in a Y, likely?

00:05:27.834 --> 00:05:29.567
-Yeah, probably. Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:05:29.567 --> 00:05:31.567
And, now, this is --
You know, again, obviously,

00:05:31.567 --> 00:05:34.000
just based on that story alone,
we know that the depth of the

00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:36.400
interesting things that
have happened in your life.

00:05:36.400 --> 00:05:40.033
You believed you grew up in
Herman Melville's house.

00:05:40.033 --> 00:05:41.901
-This is also true.

00:05:41.901 --> 00:05:44.000
-Which is an
incredible thing to grow up

00:05:44.000 --> 00:05:45.667
in Herman Melville's home.

00:05:45.667 --> 00:05:48.067
-Yes, or so I thought.

00:05:48.067 --> 00:05:49.734
-How do you get that wrong,
I guess?

00:05:49.734 --> 00:05:52.400
-Yeah, well, you should ask your
friends at "The New Yorker."

00:05:52.400 --> 00:05:56.701
Surely, you've read it.

00:05:56.701 --> 00:05:58.767
-The magazine?
-Yeah.

00:05:58.767 --> 00:06:02.400
I mean, they do this thing
there, I guess,

00:06:02.400 --> 00:06:03.801
called fact-checking?

00:06:03.801 --> 00:06:05.133
-Yeah.

00:06:05.133 --> 00:06:07.267
-I don't know.

00:06:07.267 --> 00:06:11.667
Like, who is it for?
You know what I mean?

00:06:11.667 --> 00:06:14.667
We get it.
You guys are smart or something.

00:06:14.667 --> 00:06:16.467
I don't know. College.

00:06:16.467 --> 00:06:20.133
So -- Yeah, so, I guess
they told me that,

00:06:20.133 --> 00:06:23.100
"Yes, your childhood was a
sham," which I already knew.

00:06:23.100 --> 00:06:25.667
-So, did you tell a reporter
that you lived in --

00:06:25.667 --> 00:06:27.667
-The story I was told.
-Okay.

00:06:27.667 --> 00:06:29.534
And then they
came back to you and said...

00:06:29.534 --> 00:06:33.334
-And I was like, "Well, who else
is claiming Herman Melville?

00:06:33.334 --> 00:06:35.634
And are all these people
running around being like,

00:06:35.634 --> 00:06:37.100
'I lived in Moby-Dick's house'?

00:06:37.100 --> 00:06:41.701
Like, calm down.
Give me one thing."

00:06:41.701 --> 00:06:44.901
And, so, anyway, it turns out
I did not grow up

00:06:44.901 --> 00:06:47.667
in Herman Melville's home,
if that's where this is leading.

00:06:47.667 --> 00:06:49.067
-That was my gotcha question.

00:06:49.067 --> 00:06:52.767
-If that's why you brought
me here, today, Seth, you win.

00:06:52.767 --> 00:06:54.767
Again.
-You know what?

00:06:54.767 --> 00:06:57.033
You walked in here on your
high horse, being like,

00:06:57.033 --> 00:07:00.767
"I lived in Melville's house."
and now we all know the truth.

00:07:00.767 --> 00:07:02.033
-Yeah.

00:07:02.033 --> 00:07:05.300
-Did you really watch
a couple of animals, like,

00:07:05.300 --> 00:07:07.534
meet their demise
in your home growing up?

00:07:07.534 --> 00:07:08.901
-Uh...
-Rabbits?

00:07:08.901 --> 00:07:11.834
-I did Seth, yeah.
-So, what happened?

00:07:11.834 --> 00:07:15.000
-Yeah, well, you know,
it was a rocky road.

00:07:15.000 --> 00:07:19.534
And I guess --
Well, my parents --

00:07:19.534 --> 00:07:20.901
I want to say
they were eccentrics,

00:07:20.901 --> 00:07:21.968
but they were just alcoholics.

00:07:21.968 --> 00:07:23.868
-Okay.
-And --

00:07:23.868 --> 00:07:24.934
-Were you worried
"The New Yorker"

00:07:24.934 --> 00:07:26.267
was going to fact-check it?

00:07:26.267 --> 00:07:27.868
-Yeah.

00:07:27.868 --> 00:07:30.200
And then -- So, yeah, I guess

00:07:30.200 --> 00:07:32.901
they overfed
the sheep and the rabbits.

00:07:32.901 --> 00:07:37.100
And, you know,
I love animals, so --

00:07:37.100 --> 00:07:38.234
-Do you have pets now?

00:07:38.234 --> 00:07:40.067
-Of course. Rootbeer.
I give her many --

00:07:40.067 --> 00:07:42.133
-Whoo!

00:07:42.133 --> 00:07:44.868
-Wow. I like that it's
a Rootbeer Head.

00:07:44.868 --> 00:07:46.501
But, I mean,
I give her a lot of snacks,

00:07:46.501 --> 00:07:49.734
but I would never explode her.

00:07:49.734 --> 00:07:51.400
Yes, no.
-That's how the sheep went?

00:07:51.400 --> 00:07:54.300
A full explosion of --
-That's what I remember.

00:07:54.300 --> 00:07:56.167
I don't know.
-But you remember a lot wrong.

00:07:56.167 --> 00:07:59.601
-Yes.

00:07:59.601 --> 00:08:02.868
Or too right. Too right.

00:08:02.868 --> 00:08:04.400
You know?
-It was.

00:08:04.400 --> 00:08:06.300
-Yeah.
-It was two rights make a wrong.

00:08:06.300 --> 00:08:08.834
-Yeah, it's not as easy to get
a lobotomy as it used to be.

00:08:08.834 --> 00:08:09.901
-Oh, my God.

00:08:09.901 --> 00:08:12.300
Well, I will hope you
never get one

00:08:12.300 --> 00:08:13.567
and I hope you keep coming back.

00:08:13.567 --> 00:08:15.501
And, my goodness gracious,
it's such a fun show.

00:08:15.501 --> 00:08:17.133
And it's always
such a delight to see you.

00:08:17.133 --> 00:08:18.667
-Thank you.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:08:18.667 --> 00:08:21.167
There's no one like
Natasha Lyonne, everybody.

00:08:21.167 --> 00:08:23.801
New episodes of "Poker Face"
stream Thursdays on Peacock.

00:08:23.801 --> 00:08:25.801
We'll be right back with
Adam Pally.

